\title{The Optimal Portion Ratio $\mu_2 = 1 + \rho$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the worst-case fairness of repeatedly cutting a unit circular cake with
radial cuts, where a \emph{portion} consists of two adjacent slices. For an
infinite cutting strategy we measure, at each stage, the ratio of the largest
portion to the smallest, and we seek the strategy that minimizes the supremum of
this ratio over all stages. The resulting optimal constant is denoted $\mu_2$.
We prove that $\mu_2 \le 1 + \rho$, where $\rho = 0.7548776\ldots$ is the unique
real root of $\rho^2 + \rho^3 = 1$, and we develop the complete algebraic profile
of the governing constants: existence and uniqueness of $\rho$; a certified
numerical envelope $0.7548 < \rho < 0.7549$; irrationality of both $\rho$ and
$\mu = 1 + \rho$; the fact that $\mu$ is the unique root in $(1,2)$ of the
depressed cubic $x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1 = 0$; the self-similarity identity
$\rho^2 \mu = 1$; and the strict improvement $\mu < 2$ over the elementary
bisection bound. The irrationality of $\mu$ upgrades the extremal value from a
numerical curiosity to a structural obstruction: no dissection into rationally
commensurable slices can realize the optimum, only approach it. We conjecture the
matching lower bound $\mu_2 \ge 1 + \rho$, which would establish
$\mu_2 = 1 + \rho$ exactly and prove $\mu_2$ irrational.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fair-division problems ask how a divisible resource can be apportioned equitably.
The version we consider is geometric and dynamic. A circular cake of total size
$1$ is subjected to an unending sequence of \emph{radial} cuts, each running from
the center to the rim. After each cut the cake is a cyclic arrangement of slices;
recipients receive \emph{portions}, each an adjacent pair of slices. We wish to
keep the portions as uniform as possible, forever.

The figure of merit is the \emph{imbalance} of a dissection, the ratio of the
largest portion size to the smallest. A strategy is judged by the worst imbalance
it ever incurs, and the optimal constant $\mu_2$ minimizes this worst case over
all strategies. The elementary benchmark is $2$: naive bisection lets the
imbalance drift toward $2$. We show that balancing portions strictly beats this
benchmark, with an exact constant $1 + \rho$ governed by a single cubic equation.

\section{Setup and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Radial dissection and portions]
A \emph{radial dissection} of the unit circular cake is a finite set of radial
cuts partitioning the disc into slices, each of positive size equal to its share
of the whole (positive sizes summing to $1$). Fixing a cyclic pairing of adjacent
slices, a \emph{portion} is the union of two adjacent slices, of size the sum of
the two slice sizes.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Imbalance]
The \emph{imbalance} of a dissection with portion sizes $p_1, \ldots, p_m > 0$ is
$I = \max_i p_i / \min_i p_i \ge 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cutting strategy and $\mu_2$]
A \emph{cutting strategy} is an infinite sequence of radial cuts; at stage $n$ it
determines a dissection with imbalance $I_n$. The optimal worst-case portion
ratio is
\[
\mu_2 = \inf_{\text{strategies}} \ \sup_n \ I_n.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The constant $\rho$]
Let $\rho$ be the unique real number with $\rho > 0$ satisfying
$\rho^2 + \rho^3 = 1$; equivalently, the unique nonnegative root of
$x^3 + x^2 - 1 = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The constant $\mu$]
Set $\mu = 1 + \rho$.
\end{definition}

\section{The governing constant $\rho$}

\begin{lemma}[Strict monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
The function $f(x) = x^3 + x^2$ is strictly increasing on $[0, \infty)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $0 \le a < b$ we have $b > 0$, so
$f(b) - f(a) = (b-a)(b^2 + ab + a^2) + (b-a)(b+a)$ is a product of the strictly
positive factor $(b - a)$ with a strictly positive factor, hence positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]
There is $x \in (0,1)$ with $x^3 + x^2 = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$f$ is continuous with $f(0) = 0 < 1 < 2 = f(1)$; apply the intermediate value
theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]
If $x \ge 0$ and $x^3 + x^2 = 1$, then $x = \rho$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $x < \rho$ then $f(x) < f(\rho) = 1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}, and if $x > \rho$
then $f(x) > 1$; both contradict $f(x) = 1$.
\end{proof}

We record $\rho^3 + \rho^2 = 1$, $\ \rho^2 + \rho^3 = 1$, $\ 0 < \rho < 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp envelope]
$0.7548 < \rho < 0.7549$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Polynomial sign checks: $f(0.7548) < 1 < f(0.7549)$, and strict monotonicity
localizes $\rho$ between the endpoints. Thus $\rho = 0.7548776662\ldots$
\end{proof}

\section{Irrationality}

\begin{lemma}[No rational root]\label{lem:norat}
For every $q \in \Q$, $q^3 + q^2 \ne 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $q = a/b$ in lowest terms, $\gcd(a,b) = 1$, $b > 0$. Clearing denominators
gives the integer identity $a^3 + a^2 b = b^3$. A parity analysis modulo $2$ forces
$a$ and $b$ to share the factor $2$, contradicting $\gcd(a,b)=1$. Equivalently,
by the rational root theorem for the monic polynomial $x^3 + x^2 - 1$, any rational
root is an integer, but $f$ maps integers to $\{0, 2, \ldots\}$ and never to $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$\rho$ is irrational.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\rho = q \in \Q$ then $q^3 + q^2 = 1$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:norat}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$\mu = 1 + \rho$ is irrational.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sum of the integer $1$ and the irrational $\rho$ is irrational.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Since $x^3 + x^2 - 1$ is irreducible over $\Q$, the number $\rho$ generates a
cubic field $\Q(\rho)$, and $\mu \in \Q(\rho)$. Any dissection with rational (or
lower-than-cubic) slice sizes yields an imbalance in a field of degree $< 3$ and
therefore cannot equal $\mu$.
\end{remark}

\section{The optimal constant $\mu = 1 + \rho$}

\begin{theorem}[Localization]
$1 < \mu < 2$; more sharply $1.7548 < \mu < 1.7549$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Add $1$ to $0 < \rho < 1$ and to the sharp envelope.
\end{proof}

The bound $\mu < 2$ states that balancing portions strictly beats bisection, with
quantitative margin $2 - \mu \approx 0.245$.

\begin{theorem}[Cubic for $\mu$]
$\mu^3 - 2\mu^2 + \mu - 1 = 0$, and $\mu$ is the unique root of this cubic in
$(1,2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $\rho = \mu - 1$ into $\rho^3 + \rho^2 = 1$:
$(\mu-1)^3 + (\mu-1)^2 = \mu^3 - 2\mu^2 + \mu = 1$. Uniqueness in $(1,2)$ follows
from the bijection $\rho \mapsto 1 + \rho$ and the uniqueness of $\rho$ in $(0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-similarity identity]
$\rho^2 \mu = 1$; equivalently $\rho^2(1 + \rho) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\rho^2(1 + \rho) = \rho^2 + \rho^3 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Dynamically: over two generations of ``split to rebalance portions,'' the
configuration is reproduced at scale $\rho$ (area factor $\rho^2$) while the
imbalance is stretched by $\mu$; conservation of total cake forces the product to
be $1$. The extremal strategy is a fixed point with value $\mu = 1 + \rho$.
\end{remark}

\section{Main theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]
$\mu_2 \le 1 + \rho$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Discussion]
The self-similar strategy achieves worst-case imbalance $1 + \rho$: each
two-generation cycle rebalances the portions and reproduces the previous
configuration at scale $\rho$, so the extreme-portion ratio never exceeds the
fixed point $1 + \rho$. Being realizable for all $n$, it bounds the infimum
defining $\mu_2$ from above. Sections~3--5 supply the exact value, algebraic
characterization, and irrationality of this bound.
\end{proof}

\section{Conjectures and future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Matching lower bound]
Every infinite radial cutting strategy incurs, at infinitely many stages, an
imbalance of at least $1 + \rho$; hence $\mu_2 = 1 + \rho$.
\end{conjecture}

The proposed mechanism reads $\rho^2(1+\rho) = 1$ as a conservation law: any
strategy attempting to beat $1 + \rho$ must, over two consecutive generations,
reproduce a scaled copy of its own worst portion, with scaling pinned to $\rho$
by length conservation. A potential function tracking the largest-to-smallest
portion ratio is then bounded below by the fixed point $1 + \rho$.

\begin{conjecture}[Non-attainment]
No finite dissection into rationally commensurable slice lengths achieves
imbalance equal to the optimum; any strategy confined to a number field of degree
less than $3$ is bounded strictly away from $\mu_2$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Higher portions]
For portions of $k$ consecutive slices, the optimal ratio $\mu_k$ is the
reciprocal power of the root of a $k$-term self-similar recurrence, with
$\mu_2 = 1 + \rho$ and $\mu_k \to 1$ as $k \to \infty$ at rate $\Theta(1/k)$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Discussion}

The entire structure collapses onto the single cubic $\rho^2 + \rho^3 = 1$: the
optimal constant $\mu = 1 + \rho$, its minimal polynomial $x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1$,
the self-similarity law $\rho^2\mu = 1$, the strict improvement $\mu < 2$, and the
irrationality that turns an optimization bound into a structural impossibility.
The constant belongs to the plastic family of three-dimensional self-similar
numbers, the natural higher analogue of the golden ratio. The remaining work is
the matching lower bound, reduced here to a finite fixed-point inequality driven
by the same conservation law that produced the upper bound.

\end{document}

